OSP Release Notes
Open Service Platform

!!!Release 2.0.0

The first release of POCO OSP as a repository of its own. It contains the Open
Service Platform of macchina.io; the entries below describe the changes that a
user of the macchina.io versions must know. The macchina.io versions differ from
each other, so an entry can describe a difference to only some of them. The
release also contains bug fixes and other changes that are not listed here.
<[CHANGELOG.md]> has the same entries.

!!Incompatible Changes and Possible Transition Issues

  - Build: CMake is the only build system; there are no GNU make files.
  - Build: POCO OSP requires a C++20 compiler; the configure stops without one.
  - Build: POCO is not part of the source tree. The configure pulls the newest
    POCO 2.x release (<[main]> while none exists) or the ref that
    <[POCO_OSP_POCO_VERSION]> names, or uses the POCO of a superproject; an
    installed POCO is refused.
  - Build: a superproject adds POCO OSP with <[add_subdirectory()]>;
    <[PocoProOspSetup.cmake]> is removed, and its option <[ENABLE_REMOTING]> is
    <[ENABLE_OSP_REMOTING]>.
  - Build: the <[ENABLE_OSP_*]> options select the OSP parts, also the bundles
    of POCO libraries, and the configure switches on the POCO options they need;
    an OSP option that needs another OSP option that is OFF stops the configure.
  - Build: Auth LDAP, Shell SSH, Data ODBC, MySQL and PostgreSQL, ActiveRecord,
    the Unit test runner and <[ospsh]> are OFF by default; only
    <[ENABLE_OSP_AUTH_LDAP]> enables LDAP, and it stops the configure on macOS.
  - Build: <[POCO_OSP_ENABLE_TESTS]> and <[POCO_OSP_ENABLE_SAMPLES]> build the
    OSP tests and samples, not <[ENABLE_TESTS]> and <[ENABLE_SAMPLES]>; both are
    OFF by default and follow these when a superproject adds POCO.
  - Build: the OSP shared libraries take their version from <[libversion]>
    (<[200]>), the CMake packages from <[VERSION]> (<[2.0.0]>).
  - Build: bundles are installed into <[<prefix>/bundles]>
    (<[POCO_INSTALL_BUNDLEDIR]>), not <[<prefix>/lib/bundles]>; in a Debug build
    the tools have the suffix <[d]> (<[bundled]>, <[signbundled]>,
    <[stripbundled]>, <[ccutild]>, <[ospshd]>).
  - Build: with <[BUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF]>, the configure switches off the
    bundles and the tests.
  - Build: the bundle creator is the package component <[OSPBundleCreator]> in
    <[<libdir>/cmake/Poco]> (<[cmake]> on Windows), where
    <[PocoOspBundle.cmake]> is also installed; <[BundleCreatorTargets.cmake]> is
    removed.
  - Build: the OSP libraries pass on only the libraries that their public
    headers use, for example no longer Zip, JSON, SSH or the LDAP library, and
    <[Poco::OSPWebEvent]> no longer <[Poco::OSPWeb]>; a target that uses them
    links them itself.
  - Build: <[poco_osp_bundle()]> stops the configure for an unknown argument and
    for a <[$<TARGET_FILE:...>]> in <[CODE]> whose target is defined after the
    call.
  - Build: <[poco_remote_gen()]> takes <[GEN_DIR_NAME]> relative to
    <[CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR]>.
  - Build: <[poco_remote_gen()]> runs RemoteGenNG in a directory of the build,
    so a configuration names its headers with <[${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}]> or
    <[${POCO_OSP_BASE}]>, not <[${POCOPLUS_BASE}]>.
  - OSP: <[Bundle::moduleProviders()]>, <[Bundle::resolvedDependencies()]> and
    <[ServiceRegistry::services()]> return a copy instead of a reference.
  - OSP: <[ServiceImpl]> does not add a <[_1]>, <[_2]> suffix to the default
    service name of further instances; instances registered at the same time
    need different names.
  - Auth: <[AuthAdminService]> and <[AbstractLDAPAuthAdminService]> declare
    further pure virtual functions; <[AuthAdminService::attributesForUser()]>
    with a map returns <[int]>, and <[escapeLDAP()]> moved to <[LDAPClient]>.
  - Auth: in the <[ACCOUNT]> mode with the <[USER]> bind mode, a login is
    refused when the LDAP entry found for the name does not have that name as
    its <[sAMAccountName]>, <[DOMAIN\name]> or <[userPrincipalName]>.
  - Auth/Data: for subclasses of <[AuthAdminServiceImpl]>, <[addUserImpl()]>
    takes the password hash, and <[authenticateDB()]> is replaced by
    <[checkPassword()]>.
  - Auth/Data: <[setUserAttribute()]> and <[removeUserAttribute()]> refuse
    attributes whose name starts with <[auth_]>; <[setAuthAttribute()]> and
    <[removeAuthAttribute()]> change them.
  - Auth/Data: <[addUser()]> and <[changePassword()]> refuse an empty password,
    and <[auth add user]> requires the password.
  - Auth/Data: the SQL user database needs the tables <[auth_role_attributes]>
    and <[auth_role_mappings]> of <[createdb.sql]>; the default
    <[auth.sql.effectivePermissionsForUser]> reads <[auth_role_mappings]> and
    takes the user name three times.
  - Auth/Data: the setting <[auth.sql.verifyPassword]> is not used; the service
    checks the password against the stored hash.
  - Auth/Data: when the LDAP server cannot be used, the stored password of an
    LDAP user is accepted only with <[auth.ldap.inaccessible.fallbackToLocal]>
    and within <[auth.ldap.cache.lifetimeDays]> of the last LDAP login.
  - Auth/Data: an LDAP user is stored under the <[sAMAccountName]> of its LDAP
    entry, in lower case, when the entry has one; <[authenticate()]> and
    <[verifyCredentials()]> return false for another name, such as the user
    principal name.
  - Auth/TwoFactor: after <[auth.twofactor.maxFailures]> wrong codes (default 5;
    0 disables the lock-out), the user is locked out for
    <[auth.twofactor.lockOutPeriod]> seconds (default 600), also a user of
    <[auth.neverLockOutUsers]>.
  - Auth/TwoFactor: the secrets are stored with AES-256-GCM. A secret in the old
    format is stored again in the new format after the first accepted code;
    macchina.io versions cannot read the new format.
  - Auth/TwoFactor: the key is derived from the passphrase and a key salt
    (<[auth.twofactor.keySalt]>, otherwise generated at the first start and kept
    in the bundle preferences); instances that share one SQL user database need
    the same salt.
  - Auth/Remoting: <[Authenticator]> and <[Authorizer]> take the identity from
    <[RequestIdentity::current()]> and ignore the <[X-OSP-Authorized-*]>
    headers.
  - Auth/AuthCommand, BundleAdmin, Shell: the <[auth]> command, and <[passwd]>
    for another user, check <[authAdmin]>; BundleAdmin and the bundle commands
    of the shell check <[bundleAdmin]>; <[loglevel]> checks <[logAdmin]>.
  - Web: <[WebServerDispatcher::addFilter()]> takes the bundle of the filter
    factory, <[WebSessionStore::saveTimeout()]> is pure virtual, and some
    functions of <[MediaTypeMapper]> and <[WebSession]> return a copy instead of
    a reference.
  - Web: a <[permission]> with several comma-separated permissions is granted to
    a user who has any one of them.
  - Web: valid credentials, or the session of a logged-in user, without the
    permission are answered with 403 instead of 401 where no redirect applies.
  - Web: on a path with a request handler, a failed authorization is answered
    with 401 or 403 and a JSON body; only a GET request for a path without a
    request handler is redirected to <[redirectOn401]>.
  - Web: on a path that redirects, a GET request in the session of a logged-in
    user without the permission is redirected to <[/]> instead of the login
    page; at <[/]> itself it is answered with 403.
  - Web: the redirect to <[redirectOn401]> adds the query parameter <[redirect]>
    with only the path and query of the request, unless the request is for the
    login page or already has a <[redirect]> parameter.
  - Web: a path without a <[session]> attribute that accepts bearer tokens but
    not HTTP Basic credentials answers a failed authentication with 401 and a
    Bearer challenge instead of a Basic challenge.
  - Web: a path that requires a permission is answered with 503 while no
    authentication service is available (bundle message <[message.unavailable]>;
    <[message.nohandler]> is removed).
  - Web: with CORS enabled, an empty <[osp.web.cors.allowedOrigin]> or
    <[allowOrigin]> allows no other origin instead of every origin with
    credentials, and <[allowOrigin]> <[*]> is sent without
    <[Access-Control-Allow-Credentials]>.
  - Web: with CORS enabled, a request from an origin that is not allowed is
    answered with 403 instead of 405 for a preflight or a method other than GET
    and HEAD, and without CORS headers for GET and HEAD.
  - WebEvent: <[WebEventService]> declares further pure virtual functions; the
    constructors of <[WebEventServiceImpl]> and <[WebEventRequestHandler]> and
    the protected functions of <[WebEventServiceImpl]> are changed.
  - WebEvent: a WebSocket upgrade whose <[Origin]> is neither the server's own
    nor listed in <[osp.web.event.allowedOrigins]> is answered with 403.
  - WebEvent: a <[NOTIFY]> message from a client is passed on only with
    <[osp.web.event.clientNotify]> (default false).
  - WebEvent: a subscriber is removed when it subscribes to more than
    <[osp.web.event.maxSubjects]> subjects (default 64) or sends nothing, not
    even a PONG, for twice <[osp.web.event.pingInterval]> (default 60 s).
  - WebEvent: delivery to a subscriber that falls behind is lossy: beyond
    <[osp.web.event.maxPendingFrames]> queued frames, the oldest notification
    frame is dropped.
  - Shell: <[CommandProcessor::COMMAND_LOGIN]>, <[COMMAND_HELP]> and
    <[COMMAND_QUIT]> are removed; the <[ShellConnection]> and
    <[ShellConnectionFactory]> constructors take a <[ShellConnection::Config]>
    and a <[SessionTracker]>.
  - Shell: before a login, only <[login]>, <[help]> and <[quit]> run; every
    other command, also one registered without a permission, is denied.
  - Shell: when the authentication service that <[authServiceName]> names
    (default <[osp.auth]>) is not registered, a password login is rejected; an
    empty <[authServiceName]> accepts every login.
  - Shell: a session is closed when no command arrives within <[sessionTimeout]>
    seconds (default 600; 0 disables it) or when a command line is longer than
    4096 characters.
  - Shell: a rejected login is answered after a delay that grows per client
    address from 1 s to 16 s, three rejected logins close the connection, and
    <[maxConnections]> defaults to 4 instead of 1.
  - Shell: the bundle <[com.appinf.osp.shell]> has the version 2.0.0, so a
    bundle that requires it with a range that ends at 2.0.0, such as
    <[[1.1.0,2.0.0)]>, does not resolve.
  - BundleAdmin: a request that changes a bundle must be a POST request whose
    <[Origin]> or <[Referer]> header names the server, and in a web session it
    must carry the CSRF token of the session (otherwise 403).
  - BundleSign: <[BundleSigner]> and <[signbundle]> sign with SHA256 by default;
    <[BundleVerifier]> refuses a SHA1 signature unless <[setAllowSHA1()]> allows
    it (<[signbundle --allow-sha1]>).
  - BundleSign: <[BundleVerifier]> accepts only a signer certificate that is
    valid at the time of the check and allows code signing.
  - BundleSign: <[signbundle --verify]> needs <[--certificate]> or <[--ca-file]>
    and does not trust the certificate in the bundle.
  - Unit: the test runner runs the tests that a bundle registers under its
    symbolic name, not under its name; <[Poco/OSP/Unit/TestCaller.h]> does not
    define the macro <[error()]>.
  - Mail: the <[MailDeliveryServiceImpl]> constructor does not start the
    delivery thread; <[start()]> starts it, and <[stop()]>, which the destructor
    calls, stops it.
  - SecureMail: the server certificate and, unless
    <[osp.mail.tls.extendedCertificateVerification]> is false, its host name are
    verified; TLS 1.2 or newer is required; only <[osp.mail.tls.*]> applies, not
    <[openSSL.client]>.
  - SecureMail: a server that refuses STARTTLS fails the delivery; the login and
    the message are not sent unencrypted.
  - JWT: a token without a subject (<[sub]>) is rejected.
  - Prometheus: the request metrics are named <[osp_web_requests_total]>,
    <[osp_web_requests_by_status_total]> and
    <[osp_web_request_duration_seconds]>.
  - Prometheus: the web server metrics have the label <[server]> instead of
    <[host]>, <[port]> and <[protocol]>.
  - Prometheus: the <[osp_web_buffer_pool_*]> gauges and the jemalloc metrics
    are removed.
  - RemotingNG: the TCP and REST bundles require the bundle <[poco.remotingng]>,
    which contains the POCO RemotingNG library.
  - JS: the OSP JS library and the JavaScript bundles (<[com.appinf.osp.js]>,
    <[com.appinf.osp.js.*]>) are not part of POCO OSP.

!!New Components

  - Auth/Remoting: a RemotingNG authenticator and authorizer that use the user
    authenticated by the OSP web server.
  - Redis: the bundle <[poco.redis]> with the POCO Redis library.
  - RemotingNG: the bundle <[poco.remotingng]> with the POCO RemotingNG library.


!!!Release 2024.1

!!Summary of Changes

  - The authentication framework includes a new Poco::OSP::Auth::PooledAuthAdminService
    class that supports database connection pooling for high-load servers.
  - The OSP web server supports a new `redirectOn401` attribute that will redirect
    to the specified location instead of sending a `401` response due to an
    authentication failure.
  - Log messages for full request logging have been improved by indenting the 
    HTTP request and response details. Also, the request details are logged as soon as
    the request has been received.
  - The Bundle Creator tool (`bundle`) supports a new `strip` option, allowing
    it to strip debug information from shared libraries before copying them
    into the bundle.
  - Various minor fixes and improvements.

!!Known Issues

  - The CMake build system places all generated bundle files in the same target
    directory, regardless of the selected build configuration (e.g., Debug, Release).
    Therefore, building different configurations in the same build directory is
    not recommended. Furthermore, on Windows, bundles built with CMake do not contain
    PDB files, unlike those built with the included Visual Studio project files.

!!Incompatible Changes and Possible Transition Issues

  - None at this time.


!!!Release 2023.1

!!Summary of Changes

  - Improved authentication service with more secure password hashing
    (supporting PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA256).
    Furthermore, it's possible to configure a session timeout, which is
    useful for MySQL servers that close the connection after a time of
    inactivity. 
  - The username and password for the OSP Shell Client can now also be given
    in environment variables `OSPSH_USERNAME` and `OSPSH_PASSWORD`.
  - Various minor internal improvements.

!!Known Issues

  - The CMake build system places all generated bundle files in the same target
    directory, regardless of the selected build configuration (e.g., Debug, Release).
    Therefore, building different configurations in the same build directory is
    not recommended. Furthermore, on Windows, bundles built with CMake do not contain
    PDB files, unlike those built with the included Visual Studio project files.

!!Incompatible Changes and Possible Transition Issues

  - None at this time.


!!!Release 2022.1

!!Summary of Changes

  - Added project and solution files for Visual Studio 2022.
  - AuthAdminService: Fixed an issue in checkDatabaseConnectivity() which would
